    'permit_when_required' => 'Per the Village FAQ: "new buildings, additions to existing structures, renovations, demolitions, and temporary buildings usually require building permits and inspections." Less obvious projects that may require a permit include swimming pools, decks, fences, and roofs, plus "some tasks that involve electrical, plumbing, heating, ventilation and air conditioning systems, and fireplaces." The Village\'s published rule of thumb: "if it involves adding, moving, or exposing water, gas, electricity or walls, or it involves your property lines, it is going to need a permit!" The Village publishes permit supplements specifically for Kitchen remodels, Bathroom remodels, Basement Buildouts, plus decks, egress windows, HVAC, roofs, fences, sheds, generators, pools, etc. — all permit-required scopes. A list of exempt cosmetic work (paint, flooring, etc.) is not published on the pages read. FAQ also notes homeowners must apply "whether a professional or a do-it-yourselfer does the job."',
    'application_process' => 'Online via the Village\'s Citizenserve Online Portal (linked as "Online Portal" from the Building Permit page; www.citizenserve.com/mpportal, also reachable via www.mountprospect.org/permits) — upload required documents; or in person at the Building Division, 2nd floor of Village Hall, Community Development, 50 S. Emerson St. Required with the application (per kitchen/bathroom/basement supplements): application completed and signed by the homeowner; signed copy of the contractor\'s estimate (or a detailed written scope/material list if the homeowner does the work); a detailed dimensioned plan (hand-drawn acceptable; cabinet-maker layout acceptable for kitchens); electrical devices/circuits marked on the plan plus a photo of the open electrical panel; plumbing isometric drawing for basement plumbing work. Removal of any wall requires architecturally stamped plans or a stamped architect letter stating whether the wall is load-bearing (and the required support if it is); "Structural changes require architectural stamped plans." Main permit page: "some residential projects will require an Architect or a Licensed Professional." A free pre-submittal meeting can be scheduled at (847) 870-5675 or permit@mountprospect.org. Building Division hours: Mon-Fri 7:30 a.m.-5 p.m.',
    'review_time' => 'not published (no plan-review turnaround time appears on the Building Permit, Fee Code, or FAQ pages read)',
    'inspections' => 'Permit applicant is responsible for scheduling ALL inspections; minimum 24 hours advance notice (48+ hours during construction season) at (847) 870-5675 option 2. Published "Residential Required Inspections and Testing" list (not exhaustive; scope-dependent): soils report (new construction/demolition/unusual sites); steel test reports; pre-pour inspections (footings, foundation walls, slabs, flatwork, driveways); concrete/steel test reports; water and sewer inspection prior to backfill; backfill inspection (incl. damp-proofing and drain tile); plumbing stack test and gas pressure test; rough inspection after all structural/mechanical/electrical/plumbing/fireplace/fire-suppression work but before walls are covered; fire suppression hydro test; electric service inspection before energizing; A/C inspection; insulation inspection after fire stopping; masonry inspection; and final inspections (roof, HVAC, plumbing and water heaters, structural, site concrete, fire suppression/fire alarm acceptance test, zoning, engineering, sewer & water service, etc.). Basement projects with plumbing also require an end-of-project water pressure test; WaterSense fixture labels are checked at final inspection (Village Ordinance 6209). FAQ: inspections must be scheduled at least 24 hours ahead and failed items get a written correction list.',
    'fees' => 'Published flat-fee tiers on the Residential Fee Code page (flat fees replace construction-cost-based fees): $75 each for fences, roofs and sheds; $200 (with $100 refundable) for decks, concrete pavement, and simple mechanical, electrical or plumbing projects; $500 ($150 refundable) for kitchen remodels or to finish a basement; $750 ($250 refundable) for full interior remodels or fire restorations. Additions and new construction: $2 per square foot of new construction, minimum fee $750, plus a $350 refundable escrow; interior remodel associated with an addition: $1 per square foot. Contractor license fee: $200.',
    'contractor_registration' => 'Yes — Village contractor licensing is required for "all Contractors that are general or specialized trade"; a company that is the only contractor on a project or oversees multiple trades is considered a General Contractor. Licenses are applied for or renewed electronically through the Online Portal (Citizenserve); fee is $200.00 and licenses are valid one year from date of issuance. State-licensed roofers, fire alarm, sprinkler, plumbing, and electrical contractors must submit their required state licenses/registrations and complete the Village application. Printed applications are accepted in person at the Building Division (cash, check, or credit card).',
    'notable_quirks' => '(1) Refundable escrow built into permit fees ($100-$350 depending on tier) — functions like a completion deposit returned after the project. (2) Conduit requirement: all new electrical wiring must be in conduit (non-metallic sheathed cable prohibited; BX not allowed in basement buildouts) — a Chicago-area quirk that affects remodel pricing. (3) Wall removal always needs an architect\'s stamped plans or stamped load-bearing determination letter. (4) Homeowner-performed plumbing triggers an Illinois State Plumbing Code restriction on selling the home for 6 months from permit closing. (5) Permit card, approved plans, and Tree Protection Plan must be displayed on the job site. (6) Free pre-submittal meetings offered. (7) Fire-sprinkler incentive options approved by the Village Board for single-family construction projects. (8) Construction hours: 7 a.m.-7 p.m., Monday-Sunday. (9) Codes: 2021 IBC/IRC, 2021 IMC/IFGC, 2020 NEC, IL State Plumbing, IL Energy Conservation and IL Accessibility codes with local amendments (adopted Feb 15, 2022). No historic district, design review board, or bond requirement for residential remodels is published on the pages read. Note: content was read from Wayback Machine archives (Mar-Apr 2026) of the official mountprospect.org pages because the live site blocks automated access.',
    'researched_at' => '2026-07-14',
  ],
  'northbrook' => 
