    ],
    'permit_when_required' => 'Per the official site, "Additions, alterations, and new construction projects require a permit" and plan reviews are conducted for these. Specifics by project: KITCHEN — "If you are planning to remodel your kitchen, a permit is required. An electrical permit is also required for the electrical work to be performed." BATHROOM — a permit is required to remodel a bathroom, BUT "Permits are not required for countertop replacement, refacing of existing cabinets or replacement of cabinets same as existing layout with no electrical or plumbing changes. However, a permit may be required whenever bathroom cabinets are replaced or added." BASEMENT — "Building permits are required for a finished basement when any walls are being built, moved or altered and/or a change of use in the proposed area (including hanging gypsum board on walls and/or ceiling). Permits are required if any electrical, HVAC, plumbing or gas piping is being performed." ADDITIONS — "A permit is required to construct an addition on your premise." Whenever remodeling a basement, bathroom or kitchen, a Residential Building Permit must be submitted and a plan review conducted, with a detailed drawing and written scope of work. Cosmetic exceptions published: window replacement needs no permit "if there are no changes to the size and location of the existing exterior window openings and no new openings are proposed." Water heaters DO require a permit. Express Permits (no plan review) are available only for roofing, siding, air conditioner, furnace, water heater, sewer repair, and plumbing. Small improvements (decks, fences, patios, pools, sidewalks, stoops) also require permits. Land Development permits (Engineering Division) required for lot grading/regrading, retaining walls, underdrains and sump pump piping.',
    'application_process' => 'Online via the OpenGov portal (schaumburgil.portal.opengov.com) — "All residential building permits have moved to the OpenGov portal, other than demolition." Applicants create an OpenGov account and submit the Residential Building Permit application; demolition, multi-family HOA, or multi-location submittals go through Permit Services at 847-923-4420 / permits@schaumburg.com. The village is phasing all permit types into OpenGov ("one-stop shop"). Published 4-step process: (1) Online submittal — detailed drawings + written scope of work; plat of survey (legible, dimensioned, scope highlighted) when applicable; signed/approved Homeowner\'s Association Form for exterior projects if the residence is HOA-governed; single collated PDF drawing set digitally stamped/signed by architect and engineer of record — EXCEPT projects under $10,000 construction cost with no structural modifications; (2) Prescreen and technical plan review, corrections issued as a plan review letter through the portal; (3) Payment and issuance — fees paid online (credit card 2.9% + $0.30 service fee, or e-check $2.25) or in person; (4) Inspections. Applications also published in Hindi, Gujarati, Spanish, and Polish (email to permits@schaumburg.com). Construction valuation is checked against R.S. Means square-foot costs (must be within 10%); undervalued submittals may be repriced by the village.',
    'review_time' => 'Published on the Permit Services Q&A page: "Ten (10) business days is the goal for the plan review" for residential permits; peak season is spring through summer, so include a minimum of ten business days in the project schedule. "All plan reviews are processed in date order received. There are no expedited options." Express Permits (re-roofs, siding, AC, furnace, water heater) are issued without plan review.',
    'inspections' => 'All inspections require a minimum 24-hour advance notice; schedule up to 4 p.m. on the business day prior via the OpenGov portal or by calling Permit Services at 847-923-4420. Published sequences: KITCHEN and BATHROOM — rough plumbing/mechanical/electrical/framing; insulation inspection; final plumbing/mechanical/electrical/framing. Bathroom guide adds a pre-start step: "Field verify code compliance of plumbing and electrical prior to start of project by setting up a Meeting on Site once the permit is issued." BASEMENT — rough framing/electrical/mechanical/plumbing; ceiling inspection (for suspended ceilings); insulation; final framing/electrical/mechanical/plumbing. ADDITION — prepour (before all footing, foundation and slab pours); rough (framing, plumbing, electrical, mechanical); insulation; active roofing; final. Construction must remain accessible and exposed until approved by the inspector; the printed permit card and approved-as-noted permit set must be visible/available on site; J.U.L.I.E. (811) must be called at least 48 hours before any digging.',
    'fees' => 'Published per-project fee for kitchen alterations, bathroom alterations, basement alterations, AND additions: "1% of construction value. $100 minimum, and $4,600 maximum fee." Complete fee schedule is in Village Code Title 3 Ch. 41 §41.01 (Municode link from the village site). Online payment service fees: credit card 2.9% + $0.30; e-check $2.25. Out-of-town Contractor\'s Business License: $125/year (includes required $10 Schaumburg Business Association fee), prorated to $67.50 July–November.',
    'contractor_registration' => 'Yes — "A Contractor\'s Business License is required for all contractors performing work in the Village of Schaumburg" before a permit can be issued. "There is no bond or insurance required." Out-of-town contractors receive an "Out of Town Contractors License" ($125/year incl. $10 SBA fee; prorated $67.50 July–Nov; valid through Dec. 31; renewals open ~Nov. 15). Contractors based in Schaumburg apply for a regular Business License or Home Based Business License instead. Exception: plumbers and roofers are licensed by the State of Illinois, not the village — current copies of state licenses must be submitted prior to permit issuance. All plumbing permit applications must include a letter of intent on the licensed plumber of record\'s business stationery with signature and corporate seal (or notarized if not incorporated), per 225 ILCS 320/37.',
    'notable_quirks' => '(1) Olde Schaumburg Centre (OSC) Historic District around Schaumburg Rd/Roselle Rd: design within the district and designated landmark buildings is evaluated under the OSC Design Manual (adopted 2005); the village publishes an OSC Architectural Resource Guide and lists local landmark and contributing buildings. (2) No contractor bond or insurance is required — unusual for Chicago suburbs. (3) Anti-lowball valuation rule: submitted construction cost must be within 10% of R.S. Means published square-foot costs or the village may reprice it / demand certified proposals. (4) HOA overlay: exterior projects on HOA-governed residences require the village\'s Homeowner\'s Association Form approved on HOA letterhead. (5) Architect/engineer digital seal required on all sheets except projects under $10,000 with no structural work. (6) Formal written code-interpretation appeal process (Village Ordinance §153.03.1). (7) Village Hall is temporarily at 1000 E. Woodfield Rd. (\\"Temporary Village Hall\\"). (8) Permit software is mid-migration: residential permits are live on OpenGov; other types are being phased in. METHOD NOTE: villageofschaumburg.com blocks automated access (Akamai 403), so all pages were read via Internet Archive snapshots of the official pages dated Feb 1 – May 16, 2026; verify current fees against the live site or Municode §41.01 before publishing.',
    'researched_at' => '2026-07-14',
  ],
  'wilmette' => 
